From ae229ac429d8a9e14192d9a61d206eb4e4ba9f3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:19:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10572/11362] Record why the supervised run reports nothing,
 and what a death would cost

Hourly check found the run healthy at 2h17m but silent, and I could not tell
"the supervisor has not fired" from "firings are never logged". Read the source
rather than the log: arc_trajectory_supervisor.py has no logger call and no
print, so grep over a run log returns 0 either way. Any hourly claim of zero
firings would have been false precision drawn from an instrument that cannot
report.

Second fact, found while sizing the risk: in scripts/arc_leaderboard_eval.py the
_write_json_atomic call sits at the same indentation as `for game in games:`, so
it runs AFTER the loop. One artifact lands after every game finishes. This run
covers two games at roughly 5.4 hours each (measured on cd82, 19,543s), so it
banks nothing for about 11 hours and a death at hour 10 loses both games'
receipts. The death receipt records stage and game names, not arm outcomes, so it
does not cover the gap.

Considered and deliberately not done: moving the write inside the per-game loop.
It is small and safe, but the running process imported the module at start, so it
cannot help this run, and editing eval code unattended at 02:20 adds risk for no
benefit. It belongs between runs.

The prior in-flight block already recorded the write-at-end half for a different
run. It is kept and marked superseded rather than replaced, per never-prune.

